Abstract
Purification of the \(C_{8}\) alkylaromatics \(o\) -xylene, \(m\) -xylene, \(p\) -xylene, and ethylbenzene remains among the most challenging industrial separations, owing to the similar shapes, boiling points, and polarities of these molecules. Herein, we report the evaluation of the metal–organic frameworks \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (dobdc4− = 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) ( \(m\) -dobdc4− = 4,6-dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate) for the separation of xylene isomers using singlecomponent adsorption isotherms and multi-component breakthrough measurements. Remarkably, \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) distinguishes among all four molecules, with binding affinities that follow the trend \(o\) -
xylene \(>\) ethylbenzene \(>\) \(m\)-xylene \(>\) \(p\)-xylene. Multi-component liquid-phase adsorption measurements further demonstrate that \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) maintains this selectivity over a wide range of concentrations. Structural characterization by single-crystal X-ray diffraction reveals that both frameworks facilitate the separation through the extent of interaction between each \(\mathrm{C}_{8}\) guest molecule with two adjacent cobalt(II) centers, as well as the ability of each isomer to pack within the framework pores. Moreover, counter to the presumed rigidity of the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) structure, \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) exhibits an unexpected structural distortion in the presence of either \(o\)-xylene or ethylbenzene that enables the accommodation of additional guest molecules.
