A portion of the crystal structures of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) (dobdc\(^{4-}\) = 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate; \(m\text{-dobdc}^{4-} = 4,6\)-dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate); purple, red, gray, and white spheres represent Co, O, C, and H atoms, respectively.
Single-component vapor-phase \( o \)-xylene (yellow), ethylbenzene (green), \( m \)-xylene (blue), and \( p \)-xylene (red) adsorption isotherms for \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) (a) and \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) (b) at \( 150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). Multi-component vapor-phase breakthrough measurements for an equimolar mixture of \( o \)-xylene (yellow), ethylbenzene (green), \( m \)-xylene (blue), and \( p \)-xylene (red) vapor with \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) (c) and \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) (d) at \( 125\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). To facilitate comparisons between the two breakthrough experiments, time is normalized by assigning the time of \( p \)-xylene breakthrough as \( t_{0} \).
Figure 3.
Multi-component liquid-phase \(o\)-xylene (yellow), ethylbenzene (green), \(m\)-xylene (blue), and \(p\)-xylene (red) adsorption measurements for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at \(33\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) using equimolar solutions of the four isomers in \(n\)-heptane. Data points with error bars (for measurements with initial \(\mathrm{C}_{8}\) isomer concentrations of 0.010, 0.050, 0.10, 0.50, 1.0, and \(1.7\,\mathrm{M}\)) were determined from an average of three replications. The error bars for data points obtained from measurements with an initial concentration of \(0.010\,\mathrm{M}\) are smaller than the markers.
Figure 4.
Structural distortion of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) upon adsorption of \(\sigma\)-xylene as determined by single-crystal X-ray diffraction at \(100\,\mathrm{K}\). Three out of four channels distort to accommodate an additional equivalent of \(\sigma\)-xylene. The \(\sigma\)-xylene molecules in the undistorted pore were found to be disordered over two sets of locations due to the \(\overline{3}\) symmetry of the framework, but only one set is shown here for clarity. Purple, red, gray, and white spheres represent Co, O, C, and H atoms, respectively.
Figure 5.
Comparison of the \(o\)-xylene adsorption isotherms for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (blue) and \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (red). The saturation capacity at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) corresponds well with full crystallographic occupancy of all the \(o\)-xylene sites in the structure of \(o\)-xylene in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at \(100\,\mathrm{K}\), whereas the capacity at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) matches a loading of one \(o\)-xylene per two cobalt sites in an undistorted pore.
Figure 6.
A portion of the structures of \(o\)-xylene, ethylbenzene, \(m\)-xylene, and \(p\)-xylene in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at \(100\;\mathrm{K}\) as determined through analysis of single-crystal X-ray diffraction data, showing the interactions of each isomer with two exposed cobalt(II) sites and the linker arene ring. The structures shown for \(o\)-xylene and ethylbenzene correspond to binding sites within the distorted hexagonal channels. Two additional binding sites were located for \(o\)-xylene (Figure S2). Purple, red, gray, and white spheres represent Co, O, C, and H atoms, respectively.
Figure 7.
A portion of the structures of \(o\)-xylene and ethylbenzene in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) at \(100\,\mathrm{K}\) as determined through analysis of single-crystal X-ray diffraction data, showing the interactions of each isomer with two exposed cobalt(II) sites and the linker arene ring. The \(\mathrm{C}_{8}\) isomers in both structures are disordered over two positions due to the mirror symmetry of the framework. Water was found to contaminate \(30\%\) of the cobalt(II) sites in the structure of ethylbenzene in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc), but only ethylbenzene is shown here for clarity. Purple, red, gray, and white spheres represent Co, O, C, and H atoms, respectively.
